{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on dbecbbf0 for the cutover head (05d618e2). Your proposal\nis within 4f697a7a. It is the read of item 3 served to a replay,\nand the cutover already changes the wire. Build it.\n\nConditions:\n1. The retained kind `birth` joins the contract's retained-kind\n   union and the signed read argument. On the route, the digest\n   position of a `birth` read holds the genesis digest of the\n   foreign sequence. State in the contract and in the wire\n   reference that this value is an entry hash and not a content\n   digest.\n2. The server may resolve the row by the genesis digest alone.\n   The reader checks the bytes with `admitSequenceGenesis` against\n   the full `at` of the use, with scope and genesis. A row that\n   fails the check reads as a damaged retained input reads today.\n3. A `birth` read takes the same permission as an `entry` read of\n   a retained input on that scope. It serves only a row that the\n   scope retains for its own uses. It gives no reader a row that\n   the `entry` kind refuses to that reader. Add one test for a\n   refused reader.\n4. The response has the shape and the byte cap of the other\n   retained kinds. An absent row gives `incomplete` with today's\n   reason, as you propose.\n5. An archived scope serves the association. The archive check\n   counts one birth dependency for each sequence that a held\n   entry uses at a later entry.\n6. The note lists the route, the new kind, and each vector or\n   wire witness that changes.\n\nThe lane's order of other work is accepted: the 22 derive\nfailures, the 21 platform failures, manifest-tree, and the\nreservation re-record.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}